WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.292 --> 00:00:03.660
(men grunting)
(punches being thrown)

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.910
- [Narrator] You've probably
seen this countless times.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.110
The shaky cameras, the fast cuts,

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:10.710
and just plain overall mayhem.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.407
It's sometimes so bad
that you may ask yourself,

00:00:13.407 --> 00:00:14.797
"What am I watching?"

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:18.590
So how did we exactly get to this?

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:19.863
Let's rewind a bit.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.120
The one common denominator
among older action films

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.260
is clarity and it makes sense.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.460
Earlier action films,
especially martial art pictures,

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.720
focused on showing the
physical feats of its stars.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.560
To achieve this, scenes were
often shot in a wide angle,

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.500
with most of the body
visible in the frame.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.250
The camera also rarely
moves and when it does

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.680
it follows the actions of its characters

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.720
to accentuate speed and power.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.630
But modern action movies
aren't shot this way anymore.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.310
Instead they're choppy, chaotic,

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:57.450
and a lot of times just confusing.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.800
When you compare these
movies side-by-side,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.080
the difference is unmistakable.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.730
But perhaps the real shock
comes when you realize

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.973
that most of this is actually intentional.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.580
So back to the question, how
did we get to this point?

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.370
Well, it's impossible to
pinpoint a specific source,

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.880
but one of the biggest influences was

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.760
probably the original Bourne Trilogy.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.400
These films were revolutionary
in the action genre,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.350
not to mention both a
commercial and critical success.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:30.750
And from jittery, hand-held
cameras to rapidly firing cuts,

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.530
you can trace all the problems in

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.200
modern action films to this series.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.110
An easy way to see how the Bourne Trilogy

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.720
changed action films is by looking at

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.800
the average shot length, or ASL for short.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.700
It's a cinematic statistic that measures

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.780
the average length of
a scene before a cut.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.410
In the first film, Bourne Identity,

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.540
the ASL was around four seconds,

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.030
which then became 2.4 seconds in

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:57.170
its sequel, Bourne Supremacy.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.910
And in the final film, Bourne Ultimatum,

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:02.500
a total of about 3,200 shots were

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.820
squashed into a 105-minute running time.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.213
That's a cut happening almost
every two seconds of the film.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.440
Another change you can
notice is in the camera work.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.480
Doug Liman in Bourne
Identity shot his sequence

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.710
more like the classic action
cinemas we're used to;

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.110
wide angles and fewer cuts to capture

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.040
the action as clearly as possible.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.860
But under Paul Greengrass's
direction the camera

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.310
becomes a lot shakier
and closer to the action.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.860
He prefers his audience
to experience the film

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.160
rather than to simply observe it,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.250
and that's what Bourne really
is, it's an experience.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:45.100
The series is actually
edited in a similar way

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.630
our consciousness and
sensory perception works,

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:52.780
how we gather bits of information
to create a larger hole.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.870
It's especially evident
in chase scenes like this,

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.470
where we see everything
from Bourne's perspective

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.320
and are given the chance to interpret

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.350
different clues to track down a target.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.730
There is an intensity
to these films that we

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.580
can't quite place and the secret lies in

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.560
how Greengrass chooses to
shoot an action sequence.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.120
Although every cut and angle seems

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.870
choppy, chaotic, and mostly random,

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:19.670
once you take a closer look at it

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:23.620
you realize that everything
serves a purpose.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.640
Not everyone dedicates the same amount

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.190
of thought like Greengrass and,

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.230
sadly, it's made for some pretty

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.760
terrible action films in recent years.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.290
There's a reason Hollywood
was so quick to adopt

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.540
Greengrass's style
because fast-paced editing

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.390
makes it easy to use visual trickery

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:42.790
to turn anyone into an action star.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:46.340
All it requires is to put
three different shots together.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:47.173
Action,

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.590
impact,

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:50.433
and reaction.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.140
This is why you often see a
cut right before the impact,

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:57.420
then a cut to the moment
after the impact takes place.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.690
They cut to hide the action.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.950
This not only makes the action look fake,

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.553
but it kills the overall
momentum of the scene as well.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.130
But in Bourne, each
cut is a deliberate act

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.820
to quicken the pace of the film.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.190
It's never really used to hide anything.

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:19.010
Every hit, every impact,
is fully delivered

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.770
in front of the camera.

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:22.480
The camera doesn't cut until the blow

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:23.910
has been fully registered,

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:28.270
making the fight more
authentic and engaging.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.290
The combination of action,
impact, and reaction

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.530
still exists in this method of editing.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:37.410
It's just not as obvious
when replicated incorrectly.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.610
And although Greengrass
does sacrifice some level

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.200
of clarity for the sake
of adding intensity,

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.770
it doesn't mean he's
abandoned it completely.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:46.260
In fact, when you watch any of the

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.490
action sequences from the Bourne series,

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:50.260
you'd be surprised to notice that

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.870
it isn't as confusing as it first appears.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:54.840
There are clever tricks at play here,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.450
if you know where to look.

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.420
First, check out the frequent use

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.130
of wide-angle shots like these.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:03.480
They let the audience understand the space

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.120
and the placement of each character.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:08.200
Sometimes in an obvious angle as this,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.000
from above as the fight
moves on to a new location.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.300
Or how most of the blows, the impact,

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.410
and brief inserts whenever a new weapon

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:19.890
is acquired are all centered on the frame;

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.520
guiding our focus, gathering these

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.943
hints of information more quickly.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:27.880
Now, let's look at bad examples.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.150
The biggest problems in films that try to

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:31.970
imitate the Bourne style is that they

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:35.200
take it too far, exaggerating everything.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.610
For instance, in this scene, you can see

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.170
the fast cuts kind of
like the Bourne style,

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.150
but it's paired with the same shot

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:44.360
repeated numerous times
in rapid succession.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.820
We've seen repetitions
in past action films

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:50.230
hundreds of times but not
quite to this extreme,

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:52.520
and let's admit it,
there's no reason to watch

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:56.510
Liam Neeson jumping over
the same fence 10 times.

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.920
Even in car chase sequences where

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.510
repetitions are bound to happen,

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:05.519
one impact is enough if shot correctly.

00:06:05.519 --> 00:06:09.540
(cars squealing and sliding)

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.550
Or even in music, most
recent films have a track

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.630
that plays throughout the
entire action sequences,

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.470
setting the pace and
sometimes fading it out

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:21.301
to accentuate key moments like this.

00:06:21.301 --> 00:06:23.968
(man screaming)

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:28.189
But it's the entirely opposite in Bourne.

00:06:28.189 --> 00:06:31.772
(intense percussion music)

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:35.490
There is no music.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:38.510
What builds momentum and tension

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:42.480
is the actual sound of the action itself.

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.390
Every sound, from the brutal impact to the

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:48.060
various tools of combat creates a rhythm,

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.043
a beat without a single note.

00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:53.111
(man throwing punches)

00:06:53.111 --> 00:06:56.654
(woman grunting)

00:06:56.654 --> 00:06:58.520
(woman being hit)

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:02.150
All these small details
and tricks make the

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:05.900
action of Bourne more
coherent and more intense

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.840
than anything we've seen before or since.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.310
It's a beautiful combination of what was

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:15.080
successful in traditional action films,

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:17.630
with a new, innovate technique.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:19.730
Bourne can't be blamed for how bad

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:21.980
action films are these days,

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.290
but the blame is on the
filmmakers who are trying

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:28.490
to replicate its technique
and failing miserably.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:32.270
Audiences are smarter than
what some directors believe.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:34.950
We can easily notice when
we're being manipulated

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:38.760
to see and feel in a way
that is not natural to us,

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:41.090
and perhaps that's what
differentiates Bourne

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:43.650
from so many of its predecessors.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.610
It knows that editing can be used

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.180
to make anyone an action star,

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:50.340
but not necessarily a good action film.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.780
And it's aware what this genre of cinema

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:55.053
is really about in the end,

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:56.933
action.

00:07:58.788 --> 00:08:01.955
(gentle piano music)

